 { Now we will see some defensive techniques that complement Philidor. First we will deal with the knight's pawn. This pawn and the rook's pawn allow an alternative and even more simple defensive technique. } 1. Ka6 { We can see that the Philidor method does not apply here. } Rc8 $1 { This is the alternative method - in this position, the only one. The king stands in front of the pawn while the rook just stays on the first rank. It is important to realise that this procedure does not work against a bishop's or central pawn, because then there is more room for the attacking side on the left of the pawn. Given that clue, the reader should be able to discover the winning procedure with a bishop's pawn. You can check it as an Exercise. } 2. b6 Rf8 3. Rb7+ Ka8 $1 ( { Black has to be careful until the end: } 3... Kc8 $2 { loses to } 4. Ka7 $18 { . } ) 
4. Ra7+ Kb8 5. Rh7 Rg8 { It is easy to see that White cannot make further progress. } 
