 { When the defending rook lacks distant effectiveness and the strong side has a central pawn on the 6th rank, the ending is won. The winning sequence is so accurate that it would be really difficult to find it over the board. On the other hand, that shows us we are in an extreme position, on the edge between a win and a draw. } 1. Kd6+ $1 { First, White must transfer the move to his opponent, as the black pieces occupy their ideal positions (apart from the loss of distant effectiveness) . The straightforward attempt to drive the black king away (that is, Rg1) to reach the Lucena Position, misses the win: } ( 1. Ra1 $2 Rb7 $1 2. Kd8 Rb8 $1 3. Kc7 Rb2 { intending ... Kf8 or ...Kf6 } 4. Rf1 { cutting off the king } Ra2 $1 { The pawn needs one tempo to reach the 7th rank, so the black rook seizes the opportunity to recover distant effectiveness and save the game, as we saw in Ending 54. } 5. e7 Ra7 6. Kd6 Ra6 7. Kc5 Re6 { . } ) 
 ( 1. Kd7 $2 { does not work either : } Kf6 2. e7 Kf7 $11 ) 
Kf6 ( 1... Kf8 2. Kd7 Kg7 3. Ke7 { is the same. } ) 
2. Kd7 Kg7 { No useful moves were available. For instance: } ( 2... Rb1 3. e7 Rd1 4. Ke8 { Lucena; } ) 
 ( 2... Kg6 { Here the king may be worse in some lines: } 3. Ra1 $1 Rb7 4. Kc6 Rb2 5. Re1 $1 { as in the main line. } ) 
3. Ke7 { We are back in the starting position, but now it is Black's turn and there are no useful moves in sight. } Kg6 ( 3... Rb1 { would relinquish the 8th rank and lose: } 4. Ra8 $1 Rb7 ( 4... Rb2 5. Ke8 Rh2 6. Ra7 Kf6 7. e7 Rh8 8. Kd7 $18 ) 
5. Kd6 Rb6 ( 5... Kf6 6. Rf8 Kg7 7. e7 $18 ) 
6. Kd7 Rb7 7. Kc6 Re7 8. Kd6 Rb7 9. e7 $18 ) 
4. Ra1 $1 Rb7+ 5. Kd8 { I consider } ( 5. Kd6 Rb6 6. Kd7 Rb7 7. Kc6 Rb8 8. Kc7 { more practical, reaching the main line. This is longer but easier. The text move illustrates another possible line worth knowing. } ) 
Rb8+ { After } ( 5... Kf6 { White wins thanks to a forced sequence full of important tactical details. } 6. e7 $1 Rb8 ( 6... Rxe7 $6 7. Rf1 ) 
7. Kc7 Re8 8. Kd6 $1 Rb8 9. Rf1 Kg7 10. Kc7 Ra8 11. Ra1 $1 Re8 12. Kd7 $18 ) 
6. Kc7 Rb2 7. Re1 $1 { Securing promotion, which would not happen with the black king on g7. } Rc2+ 8. Kd7 Rd2+ 9. Ke8 Ra2 ( 9... Kg7 10. e7 { - Lucena. } ) 
10. e7 $18 { Winning as in the Lucena Position. Summary of Section 2This section deals with those positions where the pawn has crossed the mid-line of the board and the defending side cannot reach the Philidor Position. A. If the defending king stands in front of the pawn, there are drawing chances even though the Philidor Position is out of reach. However, you must remember that rules for the central and bishop's pawn do not apply to the knight 's pawn. In the latter case, passive back-rank defence is the only option. Against a central pawn, the drawing technique is K&H. B. If the defending king cannot stand in front of the pawn, the only valid method (apart from a few exceptional positions) involves transferring the rook to the long side so that it has distant effectiveness. Depending on how advanced the pawn is, the defending side will have more or less time to arrange the ideal set-up for his pieces: 1. Against a pawn on the 7th rank, the defending rook has to deliver checks that cannot be covered by its counterpart. 2. Against a pawn on the 6th rank, distant effectiveness is enough; in some cases, the rook must not leave the back rank. 3. Against a pawn on the 5th rank there are more chances for the defender to get distant effectiveness, but difficulties may arise if the key file is under control of the enemy rook. Now you can read again the sentence that introduced this section, which is an extremely concise summary of the study of all these positions. This idea alone may serve us to find our way through these endings , and clear guidelines are the basis of correct choices. } 
