 { Vancura Defence (II) Now that we know the Vancura Defence, it is easy to apply. Nevertheless, a quick look at databases may show you that many titled players have lost this drawn position. This is a clear demonstration of: if you do not know this, you will not find it over the board. But let us come back to the interesting position we had after White's second move. What would happen if it were White's turn again? Or if the white king's position was different, for example, after 1. Kf2, preventing a time-gaining check from the black rook to get to the 3rd rank? What if Black plays carelessly and does not take his rook immediately to the 3rd rank? Or if the starting position is different? In short: how can we easily know whether the position is won or drawn? In 1950, Romanovsky carried out a thorough study on the possible locations of the white king, supposing the black pieces were on a1 and g7. As usual, he established a drawing zone, which we can see in the diagram. However, as I have already said, I am not a great fan of memorising irregular zones, as very often they are not kept in one's head for long. Therefore, I recommend that you pay attention to the right procedures in order to be able to answer accurately in any particular case. } 1... Ra5 $5 { Still enough to draw; in terms of analysis this is the most interesting move, though not the easiest one. } ( 1... Rf1 $1 2. Ke4 Rf6 { is the line we analysed in our previous example, as well as the safest way to draw. } ) 
 ( 1... Ra4 $5 { This move prevents the king from reaching the winning zone, so it is enough to draw as well. Since it hinders the white king, this is easier than the main line: } 2. Ke3 Rb4 3. Ra7 Kg6 4. Rb7 Ra4 5. a7 Kf5 { and the black king wins the race towards the pawn. First we will see what happens if Black carelessly wastes time: } ) 
 ( 1... Ra2 $2 2. Ke4 $1 { The king enters the winning zone. Let us see what happens now if Black 'wakes up' and tries to transfer his rook to the 3rd rank. } Ra5 { the rook will not find its way with } ( 2... Re2 $6 3. Kd5 $18 ) 
3. Kd4 { - The white king approaches b4. Now the rook must urgently move along the 4th rank to reach the 3rd rank - } Rb5 4. Ra7 Kf6 $5 { putting the king on the 8th rank just helps White: } ( 4... Kf8 $6 5. Rb7 Ra5 6. a7 $18 { After } ( 4... Kg6 5. Rb7 Ra5 6. a7 { – as stated in the introduction to this section, the struggle becomes a mere king race, and the white king wins: } Kf6 7. Kc4 Ke6 8. Kb4 Ra1 9. Kc5 $18 { and the king reaches b8. } ) 
 ) 
5. Rh7 $1 ( { the black king will arrive in time after } 4... Ra5 4. Rb7 $2 { as he can gain a tempo in the king race by attacking the white rook: } Ra5 6. a7 Ke6 7. Kc4 Kd6 8. Kb4 Kc6 $11 ) 
Ra5 6. a7 Ke6 7. Kc4 Kd6 8. Kb4 Ra1 9. Kb5 $1 { +-. } ) 
2. Ke4 ( 2. Ra7 { allows many options, the soundest being ...Kg6: } Kg6 3. Ke4 Rb5 $11 { . } ) 
Rb5 { The black rook is about to reach the saving rank. } 3. Ra7+ Kg6 $1 { Now the only move, as ...Kf6 would allow the white rook to reach h6 in favourable circumstances, and the back rank is prohibited in view of the manoeuvre Rb7 and a6-a7. } ( 3... Kf6 4. Kd4 Rb6 5. Rh7 $18 { . } ) 
4. Rb7 ( { On any other square on the 7th rank, the black king could also win a tempo on his way to the queenside. Instead, } 4. Kd4 Rb6 $1 5. Kc5 Rf6 $11 { , and Black has reached the correct defensive set-up. } ) 
Ra5 5. a7 Kf6 $1 6. Rh7 $5 ( { Black has to be careful now. } 6. Kd4 { leads to a race whose result is already known. } Ke6 7. Kc4 Kd6 8. Kb4 Kc6 $11 { } ) 
Kg6 $3 { Absolutely paradoxical (at first sight, as usual) . The point is that the black rook must not leave the 4th rank, due to Kd5-c6, etc, and tactical problems arise if the black king hurries to the queenside. } ( 6... Ke6 $2 7. Rh6 $1 Kd7 8. Rh8 $1 { +- is a known motif brilliantly executed. } ) 
7. Rc7 Kf6 8. Kd4 Ke6 9. Kc4 Kd6 $11 { These variations are not exhaustive, but in my view they illustrate the situation quite well and help us draw some ideas to find our way in actual games. You can play this endgame correctly with a good knowledge of the main themes and accurate calculation. Let us summarise the main ideas from the perspective of the defender: 1) The rook must go to the 3rd rank. Otherwise, the ending is lost, as the attacking king hides on a7.2) If the rook can reach the 3rd rank by means of a check, there is no need for deep calculation.3) If the rook cannot use a check, transferring the rook to its place in time is not enough. White will then have one tempo to activate his rook, and Black will have to calculate: a) some tactical tricks and b) a king race towards the queenside.4) If the rook cannot make use of a check, at least it should prevent the advance of the stronger side's king as much as possible. } 
