 { We have seen that having the enemy king cut off by less than four files is not enough for the stronger side to push the pawn to the 7th rank and take the king out of the corner. Nevertheless, there is a chance to win with a favourable set-up that allows a shelter for the king before the pawn reaches the 7th rank, as we will see in our following example. } ( { Here we have the critical position where the king and the rook support the pawn. If white is to move, } 1. a6 { wins, as the pawn is unstoppable: } Rb1 2. Rb6 Rc1 3. a7 Rc7 4. Ka6 Rc8 5. Rb8 $18 { . } ) 
 { If Black is to move, accurate play saves the game. Obviously, White 's victory is easier if the black king is farther away or the pawn is more advanced. If we keep this position in mind, we will be able to solve this kind of ending correctly, though there are many subtleties. } Rb1+ $1 2. Rb6 Rc1 { This is the ideal position for the rook to deliver checks. } 3. a6 ( 3. Rb2 { makes sense, as White threatens 4. Rd2 and frees more squares for the king: } Rc7 4. Kb6 Kc8 $11 { and the black king stands in front of the pawn or blocks the white king’s way out. } ) 
Rc7+ 4. Ka8 Rc8+ 5. Ka7 ( 5. Rb8 Rc1 { not } ( 5. Ka7 Kc7 $2 6. a7 $18 { and double zugzwang } ) 
6. a7 Kc7 $11 ) 
Rc7+ 6. Rb7 Kc8 $1 7. Kb6 Rc1 8. Rh7 Rb1+ 9. Ka7 Ra1 $11 { There is no way to make progress. } 
